  "worked_solution": "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+\u0394x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u03c4}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+\u0394y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the LTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- After the first step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.11$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.756.$ So, the LTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u03c4}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.11)-0.756 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+3(0.11)}-0.756 \\\\  & \u2248-0.004.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the second step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.22$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.597.$ So, the LTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u03c4}_{2} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.22)-0.597 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+3(0.22)}-0.597 \\\\  & \u22480.005.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the third step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.33$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.509.$ So, the LTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u03c4}_{3} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.33)-0.509 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+3(0.33)}-0.509 \\\\  & \u2248-0.006.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${\u03c4}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.11$ | $0.756$ | $-0.004$ |\n| $2$ | $0.22$ | $0.597$ | $0.005$ |\n| $3$ | $0.33$ | $0.509$ | $-0.006$ |",
